# Headers for the static build. Netlify and Cloudflare Workers Static Assets share this
# file convention (and the same semantics), so it works before and after the migration.
#
# ⚠️ When multiple rules match a path, BOTH platforms comma-join same-name headers rather
# than letting the more specific rule win — so no two rules here may set the same header
# for overlapping paths. That's why the cache policy enumerates paths instead of using a
# /* catch-all: a catch-all Cache-Control would corrupt the fingerprinted-asset rules.
# (A /* Cache-Tag is safe — nothing else sets Cache-Tag, so there's nothing to join with.)
#
# EDGE-CACHE INVALIDATION: every asset-layer response is tagged `Cache-Tag: site` below. The
# deploy purges that one tag (.github/workflows/web.yml) to evict stale HTML/feeds at
# Cloudflare's edge after a rebuild — ETag revalidation alone did NOT (the edge served
# cf-cache-status: HIT against its own stale copy). Cloudflare consumes and strips Cache-Tag,
# so it never reaches clients, and image transformations (/cdn-cgi/image/*) never hit the
# asset layer, so they're never tagged and survive the purge.

/*
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer-when-downgrade
  Cache-Tag: site

/.well-known/site.standard.publication
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

# Fingerprinted by asset_hash (config.rb): new content = new URL, so these can never go
# stale and are safe to cache forever.
/javascripts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/stylesheets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Stable URLs whose content changes every build, with extensions Cloudflare's default
# edge cache picks up (.xml, .json, .txt, .png, .ico). Store, but always revalidate against
# the ETag. Deploys don't self-invalidate here — the edge kept serving stale copies (HIT)
# against its own ETag — so the deploy's Cache-Tag purge (see the top note) is what refreshes
# them. (Extensionless HTML needs no Cache-Control rule: it isn't in Cloudflare's default-cache
# extension list, and max-age=0 + must-revalidate is already the default for it — but it IS
# tagged via /* above, so the purge covers it too.)
/feed.xml
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/sitemap.xml
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/manifest.json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/robots.txt
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/llms.txt
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/favicon.ico
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# The per-tag Atom feeds live at /tagged/**/feed.xml. This also matches the tag archive
# HTML pages, which is harmless: this directive is exactly the default they'd get anyway.
/tagged/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
